Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Heat the full circumference evenly and keep heating until the wall has completed recovery. Heavy wall parts look recovered on the outside while the inner surface is still cold.
- Let the part cool in place without forced cooling, since the recovered shape sets as it cools.
- Deburr and clean the bar, and remove any metal swarf before sleeving.
- Check the recovered wall at bends and at the ends of the sleeved length before closing the assembly.
- Re-torque the bar connections after recovery is completed.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Incomplete recovery at the inner surface | Heat applied too quickly and stopped as soon as the outer surface looked recovered | Extend the heating time, verify on a sample bar, and check wall thickness after recovery. |
| Tracking at the end of the sleeve | Open end left close to an energised part or exposed to pollution | Set the sleeve back to the drawing so the required creepage over the plain bar is preserved. |
| Split at a bend | Local overheating at a bend or a burr left on the bar | Deburr the bar and move the heat source continuously rather than holding it in one place. |
| Assembly fails clearances | Clearance calculated from the plain bar rather than the sleeved profile | Calculate with the final sleeved dimensions and apply the assembly standard. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Medium voltage switchgear busbars in substations and industrial installations.
- Outdoor switchyards where tracking performance matters.
- Mining and heavy industrial switchgear.
- Utility EPC work where the assembly has to be type tested.

